## Land a PR with the repo's own command

You can merge your own PR, and you may arm GitHub's auto-merge on it with
`gh pr merge --auto --squash <n>`: no ruleset requires a review. You have no sudo, so you
cannot deploy it. Land it with the command the repo's `land-after-merge` skill gives,
`land.sh --pr <n> --arm-merge --await-merge --detach && cc-wait land <n>`, whether or not you
armed auto-merge. As this user, `land.sh` hands the PR to `claude-land@<n>.service`, which lands
it as the operator and reports that landing's verdict and exit code as its own. It refuses
`--tags`, `--since` and `--subject`, because the unit decides what deploys.

The lander refuses a PR that changes a path on its approval list,
`/etc/claude-land/approval-paths`. Those paths widen your own authority. It also refuses a PR
that changes a module the landing code imports, such as one under `scripts/lib/`, because that
code decides whether the next PR lands. When it refuses one,
leave the PR open, tell the operator it needs their review, and stop. Once they approve the PR's
head commit on GitHub, run the same command again. A push after their approval needs a new
approval. Do not arm auto-merge on a PR the lander refused: the operator allows it, but the
refusal is the operator asking to see that change first.
{% endif %}
